  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe are to find a sequence of non-negative coefficients that minimizes the evaluation function `score(sequence) = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a))/sum(a)^2`, where `n` is the length of the sequence, `convolve` is the full convolution, and each `a[i]` is the coefficient of the sequence. The goal is to achieve a score of \u2264 1.5027.\n\nThe previous program's approach of normalization and optimization is good but relies on fixed initializations and hyperparameter schedules. We aim to improve this by:\n- Using a broader initialization strategy including more profiles (including Chebyshev-like bases)\n- Implementing adaptive dimensionality during optimization based on intermediate score improvements\n- Incorporating better termination conditions when the sequence becomes too long\n\nThe bottleneck in the previous solution was its limited exploration of the potential-f function space, focusing on linear ramp profiles and fixed interpolation rules.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe candidate sequence is represented as a normalized array of non-negative real numbers. We optimize using the linear programming (`lp_refine`) step and gradient-based (`gradient_polish`) step, but allow incremental extension of the sequence length if an iteration improves the score. Each optimization step maintains non-negativity and the sum normalization constraint. The objective is non-convex but can be approximated well by the linear programming and gradient methods.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nHere is a detailed step-by-step procedure for the search:\n\nImplementation Steps:\n1. Check if 'height_sequence_1' is available globally (parent sequence)\n2. If available, normalize it to unit sum but keep the original array clipped to [0,1000] bounds. If not, use predefined length-based initializations.\n3. Set deadlines based on total budget (1800s) and available time\n4. Initialize the search: include all parent-based candidate sequences and length-diverging sequences from scratch\n5. For each candidate initialization and its doubled lengths, run optimized refinement steps:\n   - `lp_refine` with strict early termination if sum or normalization issues\n   - `gradient_polish` with momentum and adaptive learning rates\n   - Use scientific visualization to track non-negative, bounded values\n6. Maintain the best valid candidate and its score through time checks\n7. Resume optimization from larger sequences if there's time and score improves\n8. Print progress including candidate length changes and intermediate evaluations\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nWe use:\n- Primary seeds (if parent provided) and various profiles (linear, quadratic, Chebyshev) for sequences at different lengths (powers of 2: 256, 512, 1024, 2048) but scale down long sequences for stability\n- Hyperparameters derived from past performance, considering a warm-up period\n- Strict non-negative preservation with bounds\n- Iterative optimization cycle: score-driven LP refinement followed by gradient polishing\n\nThe algorithm includes six initialization types:\n- Standard average\n- Ramp profiles\n- Linear profiles with reduced slope\n- Chebyshev basis smoothing\n- Symmetric extension from ends\n- Iterative similarity-seeking updates\n\nTermination occurs after 1500s of computation (reserving 300s for fallback) or if no improvement is seen for 100 iterations.\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 100s for quick validation and initializations\n- 700s for comprehensive optimization cycle and dimension expansion\n- 1000s for continuous monitoring and score calculation\n- Remaining for fallback and defensive programming\n\nFallback sequence is a Chebyshev-like profile: `make_ramp(t, max_alpha)` for small sequences (\u22644096) but only if intermediate evaluations are stable.\n\nTerminate on any of:\n- Time exceeds budget\n- Convolution evaluation produces infinities\n- Sum becomes too close to zero (less than 1e-8)\n- Best score plateau for >500 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nAfter every update, validate:\n- Sequence has non-negative values and at least one positive\n- Sum \u2265 1e-3\n- Evaluation gives finite score\n\n sequences are normalized to unit-sum internally before evaluation.\n\nFinal",
